Google cloud next 2017 hosted a huge  number of talks detailing the future of  cloud if you didn't get a chance to  catch the session saving money on  compute engine then stay tuned because  here's the recap the pricing philosophy  for compute engine is guided by three  concepts simplicity flexibility and  fairness we aim to make pricing as  simple as we can by offering  pre-configured products like n1 standard  that can be easily customized for your  workload similarly we know that at its  essence a VM is CPUs and RAM so another  way we keep things simple and  transparent is by not bundling other  components that create opaque SKUs that  hide the true costs in terms of  flexibility we focus on the concepts of  pay for what you use and buy only what  you need all clouds are pay as you use  but the way you count usage matters a  lot in practice in GCE we've had per  minute billing since day one because at  Google we understand that workloads  don't come in hour-long chunks we also  offer custom machine types including the  ability to efficiently schedule jobs and  VMs of any size a little over a year  after we launched this feature over 20%  of all compute engine workloads are  using custom machine types and customers  are saving an average of 19% from this  feature alone when you use our VMs  consistently it's more efficient for us  and we give those savings back to you  through sustained use discounts you  still maintain all the flexibility of an  on-demand cloud start and stop instances  in whatever way makes sense to your  workload and change your usage pattern  whenever you need we'll give you the  best discount that your usage qualifies  for automatically for predefined  instances we use a concept called in  third instances to calculate the biggest  savings that we can when we introduced  custom machine types we generalize this  model so it works even when the  thousands of possible instance shapes  that you can have with CMT if the graph  on the right is a picture of how you  actually ran a series of five different  VMs of the same size and shape it  doesn't look like you ran any of them  for very long I see what we do here is  combine similar VMs over time as shown  in the middle picture where we  reinterpret those same five VMs as if  they were three longer running VMs when  you look at those as a percentage of the  month you'll get  the max discount on one a healthy  discount on the second and the first  tier of discount on the third and with  committed use discounts you can get our  best discount levels in exchange for  telling us which resources you're  guaranteeing to use for the long term on  predefined types you can get a thirty  seven percent discount on one-year  commitments and a fifty five percent  discount on three years for custom  machine types the discounts of forty and  fifty seven percent respectively with  our right sizing features you can focus  on bringing your workload to the cloud  and once it's running we'll watch your  CPU and memory utilization and give you  recommendations to help reduce costs and  by using preemptable VMs a class of  super low cost VMS that are optimized  for short term use you can save up to  80% off the list prices preemptable VMS  are ideal for fault tolerant large scale  workloads that can handle single workers  occasionally failing or disappearing and  finally there's our autoscaler because  we don't need pre-warming you don't have  to pay for idle time while VMS are just  sitting around similarly because we can  boot VMS in about 30 seconds and scale  down in 60 seconds later you're not  paying for extra boot or idle time to  hear some real examples of how customers  spend money in the cloud and how the  Google pricing philosophy and features  described here can help you save more  money check out the full session on the  Google cloud youtube channel and for  more information on saving money with  compute engine check out the full talk  on youtube if you're looking for more  recaps on great next content make sure  to check out the rest of our playlist  and don't forget the next world tour  coming soon to a city near you  [Music]  you
 

